 \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x(x)}{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "810c6a8f-0958-52c6-b20f-d96a37359b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448362} {:answer/id #uuid "08cfa10d-b846-5e6d-bf35-9404324b2a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "810c6a8f-0958-52c6-b20f-d96a37359b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448362} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5e3b0a-5393-557f-b53b-8a481a907ef0" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449221} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88619661-430d-5de8-b84b-ec40f974019f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448364} {:answer/id #uuid "1a45e285-3045-5c04-8840-34af5a94d221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a45e285-3045-5c04-8840-34af5a94d221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448365} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cee3926-16f6-5e9e-9417-558226515e81" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449222} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e8e63f-6e04-57b4-8a7d-297ae9238284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448366} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec1f619-5c35-5d30-81b4-076f333cd284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ec1f619-5c35-5d30-81b4-076f333cd284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448367} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1158047-7645-528c-878f-9126eb72416b" :answer-field/key "The limit does not exist" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7bd92abc-0899-50b8-83be-62e32b920d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-129408" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{4}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,x=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{y}}{{0}^{4}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a49b518-1242-5538-88d2-11c15ad409a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266511} {:answer/id #uuid "47bd735c-d78a-5fa1-af23-606d585fec22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266785} {:answer/id #uuid "d7848761-d53a-5319-b2fc-3092efadcb6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268060} {:answer/id #uuid "f7519539-e4d9-5d01-ab39-32fa53d98166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268336} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6c5ae7-fa41-560e-9dd3-471731162c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{91}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7519539-e4d9-5d01-ab39-32fa53d98166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268336} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efb648d-3ab0-5adb-a826-86d01fca5115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4dcab682-c7ea-5000-bb2e-9cbc57f39eb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-136629" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=-\\frac{3}{10},$ then $cos⁡(-x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b278184-3c1a-51a7-a4b7-1998a71da8a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266667} {:answer/id #uuid "64b91be6-e764-5247-9687-cc3d8a106a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267034} {:answer/id #uuid "7501336f-7401-5639-8bba-372754b5587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267173} {:answer/id #uuid "a54f3a7b-cdee-500a-8a6d-f8beaa588cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267637} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e6fb05-9b5d-5928-bd5e-dbb48801ad3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{5x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7501336f-7401-5639-8bba-372754b5587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267173} :answer-field/id #uuid "53c4cf3f-ad58-56ca-aff5-107a809e8f19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "94671e02-a8dc-5c87-b443-151869fd52de" :question/math-academy-id "q-13732" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 5{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{3x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b72e205-86d9-5b47-bdcf-349ea88b438a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266250} {:answer/id #uuid "69ce1d76-7873-550f-a974-e3238eefea4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{130}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267081} {:answer/id #uuid "82cf1fb3-6525-538b-9699-a561c30abb9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267295} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad2c511-8d76-585e-9fd2-7ea2903024cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267386} {:answer/id #uuid "cedd122e-70d9-5696-97f0-924ad1110ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82cf1fb3-6525-538b-9699-a561c30abb9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267295} :answer-field/id #uuid "4247852f-3c47-5cdb-ab06-85feef271aed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b445bd7-4fa3-531e-a119-be633f3ce5d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-13775" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}4{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=6x$ then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration,  we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}4{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{u}\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{6})}{e}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({e}^{u}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{6})}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({2}^{6}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(64-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 63 \\\\  & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fbe1e45-425c-5d24-9a2e-b0d2bc2b4422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{100}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266423} {:answer/id #uuid "222530ab-2dcc-5d51-9809-57c96320b41e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{100}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266449} {:answer/id #uuid "5f41e2cc-d2de-56ef-80d2-9ac7568a671c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266986} {:answer/id #uuid "7daa41e8-6e90-5e70-8402-bd9770caa41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267255} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c299e5-a721-5088-944a-c9d0c0bc8ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222530ab-2dcc-5d51-9809-57c96320b41e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{100}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266449} :answer-field/id #uuid "a01b2373-b791-524c-ae21-c98d1b57c90b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6e36897b-696c-54c5-85d9-3c04b5f56902" :question/math-academy-id "q-13786" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}5{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=-4x,$ then, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}5{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{u}\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{-4})}{e}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}({e}^{u}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{-4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{-4})}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}({3}^{-4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\frac{1}{81}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\left(-\\frac{80}{81}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0102fe79-93e9-5ba1-b296-a0bd93e787fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9\\,953$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272392} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f0c826-d714-57e7-b386-45e8ae0bef7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9\\,919$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273781} {:answer/id #uuid "a549033e-8e8d-52c8-9491-acecd2160ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9\\,539$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273817} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c3a143-dea2-5d3b-b852-e9026510f39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9\\,632$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273954} {:answer/id #uuid "be1ab6b9-2ae7-5f8d-afa6-400c35f16773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9\\,827$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0102fe79-93e9-5ba1-b296-a0bd93e787fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9\\,953$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272392} :answer-field/id #uuid "21f550d2-7229-52dd-ae88-5c5a063e4164" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1467861e-7fb4-522f-8278-a2b816199885" :question/math-academy-id "q-15221" :question/problem "At an archaeological site, an object is found with $30\\%$ of its original carbon-14 content. Given that the half-life of carbon-14 is $5\\,730$ years, approximately how old is the object?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5\\,730$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5\\,730=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.3{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.3{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.3{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.3\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.3 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5\\,730} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5\\,730\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈9\\,953\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $9\\,953$ years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47a7331a-55bf-5d36-8a78-4fd1d8b74b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0157" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273000} {:answer/id #uuid "541c37eb-9b7f-548a-8a98-c41a68bead93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273104} {:answer/id #uuid "55348f30-fcc3-533d-9e36-3cf1f87ba4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273112} {:answer/id #uuid "7086f9c0-b8a2-5df9-a814-2a37c3d1491e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.433" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273354} {:answer/id #uuid "73a56f0e-34af-530f-ae30-faa1982f756c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0561" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73a56f0e-34af-530f-ae30-faa1982f756c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0561" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273377} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af11240-0758-5e75-a166-a524d1aeedc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2db230b8-dc3c-5812-baf0-4aff34353f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-15588" :question/problem "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of potassium-42 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=30{e}^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in hours. The half-life of potassium-42 is $12.36\\,\\text{h}.$ Rounded to three significant figures, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=12.36\\,\\text{h}$ we have\n\n$$\n12.36=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12.36}≈0.0561\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d52117-fe8d-523e-ab7c-8b69d3b778d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272473} {:answer/id #uuid "4872ed05-3948-52c9-8e10-8c290bcef73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273004} {:answer/id #uuid "4e09fa24-3a43-5607-af82-37743fb17fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273043} {:answer/id #uuid "5f648814-57a1-50af-86b2-a08f76c8a680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273209} {:answer/id #uuid "c3887aae-20b9-5b6d-a82b-0513e7162821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09d52117-fe8d-523e-ab7c-8b69d3b778d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272473} :answer-field/id #uuid "667d8cf5-9214-5663-8a40-187a54d0f77e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "12661d5b-e4d5-5ee9-8284-57ed504ec5ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-158047" :question/problem "If $cos⁡a=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-a) & =cos⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3277d494-09c0-555c-992e-9050eca5bf3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272839} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2280f1-d62c-5faf-9bd2-ae0bde24e84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273627} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2aed32-c14e-5b83-9bd1-a2d5262366d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273720} {:answer/id #uuid "bb90256e-8517-5324-ba4a-4704746a366c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274012} {:answer/id #uuid "d466fe9f-0c00-5b9c-a1bd-3ce0ebee318b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb90256e-8517-5324-ba4a-4704746a366c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274012} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbee3054-bcc6-57fb-b4fc-f2616c6465d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e0baa0c-c163-5abd-bc09-5322350948ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-158052" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=-1,$ then $cos⁡(-x+24\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+24\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 12) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $cos⁡(-x+24\\pi )=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275f4184-6614-5fb5-b945-c0a925615360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272735} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7df9b3-60b6-5ef6-8973-d6465d2aa3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273582} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1e5805-1c6c-5d57-b929-84303eb5f147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273626} {:answer/id #uuid "ae068c2c-1302-562e-8e3f-5924d2a8dd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273888} {:answer/id #uuid "b395db12-bd50-557e-ae1c-0cff707eab03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b395db12-bd50-557e-ae1c-0cff707eab03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273938} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea032dc8-afc8-5574-9882-b42bbb63200d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a595d8eb-425e-5670-9569-3c6cc685c869" :question/math-academy-id "q-158059" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=0,$ then $\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric over the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+18\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pi \\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
